      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char s[11];\n    scanf(\"%10s\", s);\n\n    int n = strlen(s), ans = 0;\n\n    for (int flip = -1; flip < n; flip++) {\n        char t[11];\n        strcpy(t, s);\n\n        if (flip >= 0)\n            t[flip] ^= 1;\n\n        for (int l = 0; l < n; l++) {\n            int z = 0, o = 0;\n\n            for (int r = l; r < n; r++) {\n                if (t[r] == '0') z++;\n                else o++;\n\n                if (z == o && r - l + 1 > ans)\n                    ans = r - l + 1;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", ans);\n}",
      "statement": "A binary segment is called balanced if it contains the same number of {{0}}s and {{1}}s.\nYou are given a binary string [[S]]. You are allowed to flip at most one character (change {{0}} to {{1}} or {{1}} to {{0}}).\nDetermine the maximum length of a balanced contiguous segment that can be obtained.\nIf no such segment exists, output {{0}}.",
